  "rationale": "The run produced outputs/table1.json (via step 6, table1_qob_education.R), which regresses each MA(+2,-2)-detrended educational outcome on QTR1-3 dummies for the 1930-39 and 1940-49 cohorts, exactly the Table I design. Cell-by-cell comparison against the paper is a mixed picture: the Q1 effects and the total-education F-stat replicate very closely (e.g. total educ 1930-39 Q1 -0.119 vs -0.124 =3.7%, F 25.06 vs 24.9 =0.6%; total educ 1940-49 Q1 -0.084 vs -0.085 =0.7%; HS grad 1930-39 Q1 -0.019 vs -0.019 =1%; college 1940-49 Q1 -0.003 vs -0.003 =1.7%), but many cells fall in the 5-30% band (total educ 1940-49 Q2 22%, Q3 11%, F 13%; HS grad 1940-49 F 46 vs 54 =16%; college Q2 effects 16-27%) and the near-zero Q3 cells drift beyond 30% purely because the paper's target is tiny (total educ 1930-39 Q3 +0.001 vs -0.015; college 1930-39 Q3 0.005 vs 0.002; college F 6.58 vs 5.0 =32%). The evidence_summary and replication_log both flag the cause: the shipped extract is the 'positive-earnings sample,' which differs slightly from the paper's education-based Table I sample, so magnitudes are approximate (fix_severity.json rates the only fix as a benign Stata->R port with no methodology gap). All qualitative checks required by the verification instructions pass: Q1 is negative for years-of-education and high-school-graduate in both cohorts, and college effects are small. Not every cell is within 5% (so not a full match) and not every cell exceeds 30% (so not a no_match); this is a partial.",
